[Music]  so the trump organization when donald  trump left the white house was in rough  shape you know they had just taken a  beating during the pandemic hotels had  closed they had to fire  over 2 400 workers from their business  on top of that they were dealing with  fallout from january 6th  [Music]  [Applause]  so some banks and other real estate  firms said that they no longer wanted to  do anything with the trump organizations  that figure out how to manage that  and then on top of that they were also  contending with an investigation from  the manhattan district attorney who was  just months away with from charging them  with a series of financial crimes  so all of this stuff was building up at  once and you had a lot of outsiders  looking at this picture  and saying basically this is a tower  that's about to collapse and donald  trump is gonna be left there with a  bunch of rubble  and  nothing  [Music]  so in reality donald trump is fine he  got through this period he's gotten  through difficult periods before and  he's done it again  basically what he did was he refinanced  four of his properties allowing him to  suck a lot of cash out of some of those  properties he paid down the loans on  another four of his properties handling  the debt there and then maybe most  critically he sold one of his big money  losers  the trump hotel in washington dc this  had been a property that got a lot of  attention looked like he was making a  lot of money in fact was losing millions  of dollars a year  and donald trump not only got rid of it  by selling it but sold it for an  enormous amount of money far more than  anybody really thought that it was worth  and at the end of the day  he has a cleaned up balance sheet you  know he's sitting on an estimated 375  million dollars of cash  he still has a significant amount of  debt but he's kicked the can down the  road now those loans aren't due for  years and he's going to get through this  just fine  so basically for a real estate  billionaire like donald trump you're  allowed to refinance you know whenever  you want but  unlike sort of in the political world  where you have investigators or perhaps  you know prosecutors looking at your  conduct and saying what you can't and  can't do  in this case it's banks who are playing  that role so banks are going to decide  whether or not they're going to lend to  you again the tricky thing for trump is  that after january 6 a lot of banks said  that they wanted nothing to do with  donald trump but of course if there's  one rule that we know about wall street  is there's always more people looking to  make money so if certain banks say hey  we don't want to work with this guy  other banks are going to say that's what  a great opportunity business just fell  into our lab and so they're going to  look at what his profile is and say okay  what are the value of these assets how  much income are these assets throwing  off and then how much of a complication  is it that this company the trump  organization is under indictment  actively right now and ultimately uh  several lenders came to the conclusion  that those weren't things that they  really had to worry too much about and  that they would lend him money anyways  because they thought that he would be  able to repay those debts and also pay  interest on top of it and in doing so  they would make a significant amount of  money for their banks  steven roth and donald trump have known  each other for years their relationship  goes back decades but for about the last  12 or 15 years they've been business  partners and their business partners on  two  sort of anonymous boring but big big  money-making buildings one in san  francisco one in new york city uh the  one in san francisco is called 555  california street the one in new york  city is called 1290 avenue the americas  the interesting thing  with trump's debt situation is that he  had a lot of equity  locked up in those two buildings but  because vornado owned 70 of the  buildings and trump only had 30 percent  of the buildings he wasn't actually in  full control about whether or not he  could access that equity whether or not  they would sell the things or whether  they would refinance it in such a way  that would allow him to pull cash out  but ultimately steven roth did him a  favor they refinanced 555 california  street and replaced the loan that had  533 million dollars of debt on it with  one that had 1.2 billion  that allowed the two partners to suck  out more than 600 million dollars of  cash uh trump himself you know collected  roughly 150 million dollars of cash from  that refinance  and then they also refinanced 1290  avenue in the americas replacing a 950  million dollar loan with one of the same  size so the end effect is that donald  trump has both of those loans now pushed  out until about 2028 and he's got a  whole lot more cash sitting on his  balance sheet in the meantime that he  could then deploy in other parts of his  business  [Music]  the trump hotel in washington dc was a  big problem from the start trump  organization said that it was going to  invest 200 million dollars into fixing  up the building turning it into a luxury  hotel and in exchange for doing that  they would get the right to then lease  the building from the federal government  which would mean that they had to pay  three million dollars a year to the  federal government in rent and then they  could operate the building as a hotel  but  the building was never set up to make  enough money to cover the interest on  the debt that they used to spend 200  million dollars there or the amount of  rent that they had to cover  with the federal government so  ultimately what ended up happening is  that the building just lost money for  years so amidst all of this the trumps  kind of came up with this crazy plan  which is hey why don't we sell this  money losing hotel for like a wild  amount of money you know the rumor came  out that they would be willing to accept  something in the ballpark of like a half  a billion dollars now at the time you  know most independent people thought  that it was worth somewhere between like  200 maybe 250 million dollars or  something like that and ultimately they  found a buyer who paid a reported 375  million dollars which sounds like a big  discount from 500 million dollars but  when you consider that the place was  actually worth a fraction of that it's a  huge huge amount of money that the trump  organization got to take in and it  allowed them to pay off their deutsche  bank debt that 170 million dollars that  they had borrowed to renovate the  building and it also allowed them to  take a bunch of money into their own  pockets at the end of the day  donald trump for years was able to rely  on deutsche bank they were  a really close lender of his he started  using them back in the 1990s continued  using them up until recent times  his active loans that he had with  deutsche bank one in chicago one in  miami one in dc were against some of his  most troubled properties but deutsche  bank gave him the money anyway and it  really allowed him some flexibility to  do things that he wouldn't necessarily  have been able to do had he not had a  lender who was so agreeable  deutsche bank after january 6  spread the word that they didn't want  anything to do with donald trump so all  of a sudden you have this question of  well who's going to pick up the slack  these deutsche bank loans are coming due  is he going to be able to pay this stuff  out of pocket or is he going to be able  to refinance what ended up happening is  instep the small internet-based bank  that's based out of san diego  called axos bank this is not a bank that  most people have heard of it's not a  bank that historically has done a lot of  big deals but they have done some big  deals with donald trump recently first  they refinanced trump power giving the  president a hundred million dollars  there to repay a previous loan that he  had coming due and secondly they  refinanced doral which is a golf resort  down in miami  again giving them 125 million dollars so  you could pay off  deutsche bank and continue operating  they also had an interesting involvement  in the dc hotel where they took part of  the  interest in the debt that went toward  paying for that 375 million dollar  purchase price so in three of these  deals this small unheard of bank called  access pops up and what it leaves you  with the impression is that donald trump  has found his replacement for deutsche  bank  so donald trump still has plenty of debt  he has an estimated 1.1 billion dollars  debt now remember he also has  significant assets so it's not like he's  broke or anything like that  but he does have a lot of debt that  ultimately he's going to have to pay  back the beauty of what he's done with  his restructuring is he's given himself  a lot of flexibility so now he has an  estimated 375 million dollars of cash on  his balance sheet  if people aren't going to allow him to  refinance some other loans he can now  pay more of them back he also could  theoretically just refinance again he  could of course screw it up i mean he  could you know go do something that is  you know really ambitious project that  he doesn't necessarily have money for  and all of a sudden he could be in a  tight pinch again but right now he's  sort of sitting pretty  [Music]  you

